    lx.data.ExampleData(
        text="Centrica Energy Renewable Investments Limited (CERI) now Celtic Array commissioned Wessex Archaeology to undertake this review. This review was undertaken between and April and October 2012 A total of 43 borehole sequences in the Zone 9 Irish Sea (SE Development) Area have been reviewed as part of a Stage 1 geoarchaeological desk-based assessment. The was undertaken as part of a programme of baseline data gathering currently undertaken by Centrica Energy Renewable Investments Limited (CERI) now Celtic Array for the Irish Sea Zone.. The results of the geoarchaeological review indicate Pleistocene and Holocene glacial and marine sediments present across the study area and confirm the earlier findings within this area undertaken as part of Met Mast Geotechnical Assessment (Wessex Archaeology 2011). This review makes recommendations for a total of 13 partial sequences to proceed to Stage 2 recording and sampling Corresponding Centrica Document No: SE-D-CE-073-0000-000000-011 Available dataset : Reports in pdf formats, GIS data in shapefile and geodatabase format, 3dmodels",
        extractions=[
            lx.data.Extraction(extraction_class="geospatial region", extraction_text="Zone 9 Irish Sea"),
            lx.data.Extraction(extraction_class="organizations", extraction_text="Centrica Energy Renewable Investments Limited"),
            lx.data.Extraction(extraction_class="organizations", extraction_text="Celtic Array"),
            lx.data.Extraction(extraction_class="organizations", extraction_text="Wessex Archaeology"),
        ]
    ),
    lx.data.ExampleData(
        text="The work carried out so far highlights Poole Harbour as being an anomaly in the apparent steady progressive development of Pacific oyster populations around the coast, in that there is an obvious source of larvae but little successful settlement. The mechanisms which are â??dampening downâ?? expected settlement appear to include at least one physical factor but it is likely that this is moderated by biological factors such as competition for settlement space and predation. Further work is required to establish which biological factors may be modifying recruitment success and whether recruitment is moderated during the larval stage or post metamorphosis. if the dominant controlling factor(s) act before oyster settlement it is most likely water temperature that controls the naturalised population, however top-down control by predators could be significant if oysters are surviving metamorphosis.",
        extractions=[
            lx.data.Extraction(extraction_class="geospatial region", extraction_text="Poole Harbour"),
            lx.data.Extraction(extraction_class="marine species", extraction_text="Pacific oyster"),
        ]
    ),
