    lx.data.ExampleData(
        text="2015 South East swath bathymetry dataset from the Channel Coastal Observatory Catalogue (number of records: 74",
        extractions=[
            lx.data.Extraction(extraction_class="organizations", extraction_text="Channel Coastal Observatory"),
        ]
    ),
    lx.data.ExampleData(
        text="Dredging for marine sands and gravels makes a significant contribution to the raw\r\nmaterials needed for construction work and beach replenishment in the UK. Whilst\r\nthe impact of dredging on physical and biological resources is well understood less\r\nis known about how these resources recover after dredging stops. In high energy,\r\nnaturally disturbed environments, physical and biological recovery is rapid because\r\ndredge tracks are quickly eroded and faunal communities are made up of many small\r\nbodied, rapidly maturing opportunistic species that are already adapted to high levels\r\nof disturbance and rapidly recolonise disturbed areas. Nevertheless, in many habitats\r\nwhere aggregate dredging has occurred, a return to a pre-dredge physical or biological\r\nconditions often takes years or decades if it occurs at all. In many cases a return to a\r\nsimilar pre-dredge condition may never be possible. This may be because of the highly\r\nvariable distribution and settlement of marine organisms that occurs naturally or\r\nbecause sediment composition has been altered and supports a different assemblage\r\nof animals. What may be more important to recovery is the ability of a different suite\r\nof species to perform a similar function within the wider marine ecosystem. Benthic\r\ncommunities are often important in terms of cycling of organic particulate matter\r\nand the provision of a food resource for food chains that eventually lead to fisheries\r\nproduction for example. Some recent research indicates that functional recovery may\r\noccur more rapidly than recovery of community composition. This work is however,\r\nat an early stage and further studies are required to improve our understanding of\r\nthe impact of aggregate dredging on marine ecosystem functioning and its recovery.",
        extractions=[
            lx.data.Extraction(extraction_class="geospatial region", extraction_text="UK"),
            lx.data.Extraction(extraction_class="ecosystems", extraction_text="Benthic  communities"),
        ]
    ),
    lx.data.ExampleData(
        text="Wessex Archaeology (WA) was commissioned by Fugro EMU Limited to complete an archaeological assessment of geophysical data acquired over the Sheringham Shoal Offshore Wind Farm and export cable route. WA was only required to assess data at the locations of 182 geophysical anomalies and historic records interpreted in the 2009 geophysical assessment as listed in the Written Scheme of Investigation (WA 2009).",
        extractions=[
            lx.data.Extraction(extraction_class="geospatial region", extraction_text="Sheringham Shoal"),
            lx.data.Extraction(extraction_class="organizations", extraction_text="Fugro EMU Limited"),
            lx.data.Extraction(extraction_class="organizations", extraction_text="Wessex Archaeology"),
        ]
    ),
    lx.data.ExampleData(
        text="Wessex Archaeology undertook a geoarchaeological assessment of sediments retrieved during geotechnical investigations on the site of the Round 3 Hornsea Offshore Windfarm and Export Cable Route. This comprised the offshore monitoring recording of sediments at 12 borehole locations and the assessment of vibrocore logs and onshore recording of 27 vibrocore samples from the Export Cable Route. Pleistocene sediments including the Yarmouth Roads, Swarte Bank and Egmond Ground contain deposits which are thought to have been deposited during the Lower and Middle Palaeolithic archaeological periods. The Upper Botney Cut and Holocene alluvial sediments recorded are thought to relate to relate to glacial, lacustrine fluvial and estuarine environments spanning the Late Upper Palaeolithic and Mesolithic archaeological periods.",
        extractions=[
            lx.data.Extraction(extraction_class="geospatial region", extraction_text="Swarte Bank"),
            lx.data.Extraction(extraction_class="organizations", extraction_text="Wessex Archaeology"),
        ]
    ),
    lx.data.ExampleData(
        text="These records all relate to ''Dyrynda (1987) Poole Harbour subtidal survey - IV: Baseline assessment\". A copy of the report is held at Poole Harbour Commissioners, Dorset.\r\n This survey was undertaken in 3 phases, the aim being to establish a baseline survey of subtidal species and provide a framework for future monitoring. All the surveys were commissioned by the Nature Conservancy Council. The first survey was of Holes Bay in 1982. This was followed by surveys of the southern sector (1984) and northern sector (1985). All 3 surveys used a combination of grab, dredge and dive transect sampling. Samples were taken at 120 stations across the Harbour using conventional dredge and grab sampling methods. Dive transects were also established at 49 points, covering all sections of the Harbour with species and habitat details recorded metre by metre. For recording purposes, the harbour was divided up into 16 areas for which the species lists of the three survey methods were combined. For entry onto the database, each area has been enclosed within a bounding box from which the SW and NE corners have been used as the area covered by the survey. For more accurate positional information about each survey station, please refer to the original map in the report. For each site, a single species list has been compiled which combines records from all the different survey methods used.",
        extractions=[
            lx.data.Extraction(extraction_class="procedures", extraction_text="dive transect"),
            lx.data.Extraction(extraction_class="procedures", extraction_text="Dive transects"),
            lx.data.Extraction(extraction_class="procedures", extraction_text="grab"),
            lx.data.Extraction(extraction_class="procedures", extraction_text="dredge"),
            lx.data.Extraction(extraction_class="organizations", extraction_text="Nature Conservancy Council"),
            lx.data.Extraction(extraction_class="geospatial region", extraction_text="Dorset"),
            lx.data.Extraction(extraction_class="geospatial region", extraction_text="southern sector"),
            lx.data.Extraction(extraction_class="geospatial region", extraction_text="Poole Harbour"),
            lx.data.Extraction(extraction_class="geospatial region", extraction_text="Holes Bay"),
            lx.data.Extraction(extraction_class="marine regions", extraction_text="northern sector"),
            lx.data.Extraction(extraction_class="taxon", extraction_text="species"),
        ]
    ),
    lx.data.ExampleData(
        text="Since 2007 a boat-based monitoring programme has been undertaken for the Lincs/LID6 offshore wind farm to collect information on seabird species during the pre-construction (2007-2010) and construction (2011- 2012) periods. Pre-construction surveys identified 12 key species which either occurred in (regionally) important numbers or were considered potentially sensitive to the construction and operation of the offshore wind farm. The main aim of this report is to determine whether the effects of construction related activities have had a measurable influence on the populations of each of the key species. To that end information is presented to explore changes in abundance, seasonal and spatial distribution of birds recorded within the Study Area between pre-construction and construction periods. Results from surveys during the construction period indicated the continued presence of all key species, generally at similar levels of abundance compared to the pre-construction period. However, most of these species occurred in insufficient numbers to allow for robust statistical analysis. In order to explore potential disturbance effects of construction activities, ultimately three species, reflecting high, moderate and low levels of susceptibility to disturbance respectively, were chosen for inclusion in analysis: red-throated diver, guillemot and great black-backed gull. Statistical analysis of population estimates of these three species did not reveal significant differences between pre-construction and construction periods of the Lincs/LID6 offshore wind farm.",
        extractions=[
            lx.data.Extraction(extraction_class="measurements and facts", extraction_text="seasonal"),
            lx.data.Extraction(extraction_class="measurements and facts", extraction_text="abundance"),
            lx.data.Extraction(extraction_class="measurements and facts", extraction_text="population estimates"),
            lx.data.Extraction(extraction_class="organizations", extraction_text="Lincs/LID6 offshore wind farm"),
            lx.data.Extraction(extraction_class="taxon", extraction_text="species"),
            lx.data.Extraction(extraction_class="marine species", extraction_text="guillemot"),
            lx.data.Extraction(extraction_class="marine species", extraction_text="red-throated diver"),
            lx.data.Extraction(extraction_class="marine species", extraction_text="great black-backed gull"),
        ]
    ),
