    lx.data.ExampleData(
        text="Centrica Renewable Energy Ltd (CREL) commenced construction of the Lynn and Inner Dowsing (LID) offshore wind farms in 2007. The project consists of 27 turbines at Lynn and 27 turbines at Inner Dowsing and is located 5 km to 8.5 km off the Lincolnshire coast.\r\nInstallation of the foundations and transition pieces at the two sites was completed in 2007. In 2008, the turbines were installed and construction works concluded with the installation of the export and inter-array cables. During the period from May 2008 to December 2008, the wind farms were partially operational during turbine testing and the wind farms were commissioned on 23rd December 20081. The LID projects are owned by GLID Wind Farms Topco Ltd (GLID), and managed by CREL on behalf of GLID.\r\n\r\nThis annual report, consisting of an overview and Technical Appendices, presents the results of a second year of post-construction environmental monitoring at the LID sites. The second year of post-construction monitoring is represented by the period January 2010 to December 2010 (inclusive), although the exact period varies by survey parameter (e.g. the fish surveys continued to June 2011). The results from construction phase monitoring (2007 – 2008) are presented in CREL (2009) and results from the first year of post-construction monitoring (2009) are presented in CREL (2010).\r\n\r\nThis overview report has been prepared with the following objectives: \r\nTo present a summary of the methods and findings of the second year of post-construction monitoring to the Licensing Authority in line with FEPA requirements; \r\nTo present a summary of the comparisons carried out between the findings of the second year of post-construction monitoring and the findings of previous monitoring years, in order to highlight any significant changes or trends; and \r\nTo integrate the findings of the different monitoring activities, where relevant.\r\n\r\nThe remaining sections of this report are structured as follows: \r\nSection 2 provides details of the FEPA Licence requirements with regards to post-construction monitoring;\r\nSection 3 provides a summary of the Year 2 post-construction surveys carried out;\r\nSection 4 then provides an overview of the methods employed for each second year post-construction monitoring activity, in addition to a summary of the results;\r\nSection 5 draws together the results and findings of the different monitoring activities to provide a discussion regarding the conclusions of the monitoring;and\r\nFinally, a series of Technical Appendices provide the reports from individual survey activities.",
        extractions=[
            lx.data.Extraction(extraction_class="geospatial region", extraction_text="Lincolnshire"),
            lx.data.Extraction(extraction_class="organizations", extraction_text="GLID Wind Farms Topco Ltd"),
            lx.data.Extraction(extraction_class="organizations", extraction_text="Centrica Renewable Energy Ltd"),
            lx.data.Extraction(extraction_class="organizations", extraction_text="CREL"),
            lx.data.Extraction(extraction_class="migratory marine species", extraction_text="fish"),
        ]
    ),
    lx.data.ExampleData(
        text="This brief report concerns the analysis of boat mounted ADCP data recorded outside\r\nLynmouth bay on the 27th and 28th March 2005. \r\n\r\nData from this survey can be found\r\non the “Fugro GEOS Reference No C10637”, an accompanying survey\r\ncompleted by Titan Environmental Surveys Ltd, entitled “Bristol Channel: Lynmouth\r\nSite Survey” should also be used along with this report. \r\n\r\nThe survey area was rectangular in shape, stretching roughly 4.5 km east-west and 800m north-south and was positioned just offshore of Foreland Ledge, a shallow bank approximately 1km north of Foreland point.\r\nThe two main objectives of this report are as follows:\r\n1. To select a location within the surveyed area as the best possible turbine site\r\n(highest current) and check the directional stability of the flow at this position.\r\n2. To determine the mean spring current at the chosen location",
        extractions=[
            lx.data.Extraction(extraction_class="organizations", extraction_text="Titan Environmental Surveys Ltd"),
            lx.data.Extraction(extraction_class="geospatial region", extraction_text="Lynmouth bay"),
            lx.data.Extraction(extraction_class="geospatial region", extraction_text="Foreland point"),
            lx.data.Extraction(extraction_class="geospatial region", extraction_text="Foreland Ledge"),
            lx.data.Extraction(extraction_class="measurements and facts", extraction_text="mean spring current"),
        ]
    ),
    lx.data.ExampleData(
        text="Survey and excavation of the wreck of a slate-cargo ship found in the Pwll Fanog area of the Menai Strait in July 1976. This record incorporates the September 1977 pre-disturbance survey which revealed a cargo of slates with a date range of some 800 years, the 1978 survey and excavation which revealed the well preserved timbers of the wreck beneath the slate cargo, and the 1979 lifting of a small part of the keel which had broken away from a larger section exposed in 1978. The keel type was attributed to the 1100s. Radiocarbon dating of one of the ship's timbers suggested a date of loss some time in the second half of the 16th century. The site is designated as a Historic Wreck under the Protection of Wrecks Act (1973). As such, diving on, or any other interferance with the wreck including filming, survey and excavation is a criminal offense unless a license has first been obtained.",
        extractions=[
            lx.data.Extraction(extraction_class="geospatial region", extraction_text="Menai Strait"),
            lx.data.Extraction(extraction_class="policies", extraction_text="Protection of Wrecks Act"),
        ]
    ),
