    lx.data.ExampleData(
        text="This dataset contains information on infaunal abundance and biomass from sediment grabs in Red Bay, Co. Antrim in 2004 on the RV Lough Foyle. The data from this survey was used to help support the Special Area of Conservation (SAC) site designation for Red Bay, Co. Antrim. A day grab was used to collect the samples, in total there were 20 samples taken and analysed. Particle Size Analysis (PSA) and Carbon and Nitrogen analysis was carried out on the sediments from the grab. The presence and absence of Maerl (alive or dead) was also recorded. The most abundant species found was Calcarea sp. and the most frequently recorded was the polychaete Glycera lapidium.",
        extractions=[
            lx.data.Extraction(extraction_class="measurements and facts", extraction_text="biomass"),
            lx.data.Extraction(extraction_class="measurements and facts", extraction_text="infaunal abundance"),
            lx.data.Extraction(extraction_class="taxon", extraction_text="Glycera lapidium"),
            lx.data.Extraction(extraction_class="taxon", extraction_text="Calcarea sp."),
            lx.data.Extraction(extraction_class="marine species", extraction_text="Calcarea sp."),
            lx.data.Extraction(extraction_class="geospatial region", extraction_text="Co. Antrim"),
            lx.data.Extraction(extraction_class="geospatial region", extraction_text="Red Bay"),
        ]
    ),
    lx.data.ExampleData(
        text="This geodatabase contains manually digitised GI created during the Stakmap/Fishermap participatory mapping research project. The geometries have been cleansed and non polygonal features have been converted into polygons using a 300m buffer. \r\n This feature class summarises the additional information used to describe the interviewees that reported anchoring recreational watercraft. Numbers of interviewees/grid cell are recorded and the mean, minimum and maximum active days per annum and years experience are also presented per grid cell. Individual records have been summarised onto a grid 3/4 nautical mile longitude by 3/8 nautical mile latitude.\r\n This summary data set represents the activities of all interviewees, the summarisation process is considered to anonymised the data to a sufficient degree to protect the individual's privacy. As an added measure of protection, data from interviews has only been included in the amalgamated summary/grid layers if the following conditions have been met. \r\n 1) Permission has been granted for the individual data to be used, or\r\n 2) Five or more separate interviews are represented within a single record.",
        extractions=[
            lx.data.Extraction(extraction_class="projects", extraction_text="Stakmap/Fishermap"),
        ]
    ),
    lx.data.ExampleData(
        text="The Crown Estate requested guidance from Carbon Trust and Frazer-Nash Consultancy to understand the influence of power density for future floating offshore wind farms. The focus of this study investigates inter-turbine wake effects in relation to fatigue loading and its variation as the density of turbines changed, aiming to answer the key questions below: \r\n - Is floating wind wake effect fatigue loading and its relationship to power density a key driver for floating wind? \r\n - Is there a significant difference between fixed and floating wake effects due to a floating \r\n foundations additional six degrees of freedom? \r\n - Can a baseline be established for the relationship between power density and fatigue for floating \r\n wind? \r\n The project was divided into three distinct reports, building on each prior reports findings.",
        extractions=[
            lx.data.Extraction(extraction_class="organizations", extraction_text="Carbon Trust"),
            lx.data.Extraction(extraction_class="organizations", extraction_text="Frazer-Nash Consultancy"),
        ]
    ),
    lx.data.ExampleData(
        text="The shapefile contains polygons that indicate the location of crustaceans based on available data and from fishermen's knowledge. The attribute table contains infromation that indicates gear types, seasonality, months present, importance, spawning areas and other comments relating to the location.",
        extractions=[
            lx.data.Extraction(extraction_class="undescribed biodiversity", extraction_text="crustaceans"),
        ]
    ),
    lx.data.ExampleData(
        text="One of the principal environmental concerns about tidal turbines is the potential for injurious collision with marine animals. Until recently there has been little opportunity to collect and analyse data to assess this risk. Three separate deployments of GPS tags on harbour seals in Strangford Lough and Narrows during the development of the SeaGen tidal turbine have provided the first opportunity to measure individual seal behaviour in relation to the presence and operation of a commercial scale tidal turbine. Thirty six seals were tagged over three years, in 2006 (pre-installation), 2008 (during installation) and 2010 (operation). There was no overall difference in the rate at which seals travelled up and down the narrows past the location of the turbine between the three deployments. However these average rates had wide confidence limits as a result of a wide degree of variation in the behaviour of individual seals. This uncertainty limited the use of this data for investigating differences between years. In 2010, when the turbine was operational, there was evidence that the rate at which seals transited the Narrows reduced when the turbine was on, relative to when it was off. The biological importance of this reduction is unknown at present. Over all three years the frequency of transits was higher at slack tide than when the current was running. Although visual inspection of the distribution of locations where seals chose to transit the narrows suggested local avoidance of the turbine site in 2010, high inter-individual variability limited our ability to detect any significant difference. Examining the rate of transits which were close to the turbine (&lt;25m) when it was operating gives an average rate for each seal of 3 transits per year. Scaling up to the total population suggested that 300 such transits may have occurred during the period between April and August. There were 210 emergency shut downs during this same period. The results of this study suggest that the operation of the turbine is affecting the transit behaviour of seals in the narrows and therefore the risk of seals colliding with an operational tidal turbine may be lower than would be estimated by assuming no avoidance and a spatial and temporal uniform density of animals. This series contains the survey report.",
        extractions=[
            lx.data.Extraction(extraction_class="marine species", extraction_text="harbour seals"),
            lx.data.Extraction(extraction_class="geospatial region", extraction_text="Strangford Lough"),
            lx.data.Extraction(extraction_class="geospatial region", extraction_text="Narrows"),
        ]
    ),
    lx.data.ExampleData(
        text="This layer shows the distribution of modelled data from BGS of sheltered muddy gravels, a Biodiversity Action Plan Habitat.\r\n Method of creation: Using the British Geological Survey DigSBS250 GIS layer, areas classified as 'Gravel sand and silt', 'Gravelly mud', 'Gravelly muddy sand', 'Muddy gravel', 'Muddy sandy gravel', 'Slightly gravelly mud' and 'Slightly gravelly sandy mud' were extracted. Using the draft wave exposure layer (resulting from MB0102 2E.6b), areas where wave exposure is classified as less than 3 (sheltered) were extracted. Using the draft maximum tidal bed shear stresses raster (resulting from MB0102 2E.3), areas where maximum tidal bed shear stresses less than 30 Nm2 were extracted. The remaining areas less than 2kms from shore were designated sheltered muddy gravels. \r\n This layer forms one of a set of data layers created for the Defra MB0102 contract. This work will support the delivery of a network of Marine Protected Areas as required to meet existing international and national obligations and commitments, including Marine Conservation Zones (MCZs), a new measure to be delivered as part of the Marine and Coastal Access Bill, and equivalent measures under Scottish legislation. The availability of these data layers will also be of importance in underpinning Marine Planning (e.g. licensing) in our marine area.",
        extractions=[
            lx.data.Extraction(extraction_class="policies", extraction_text="Scottish legislation"),
            lx.data.Extraction(extraction_class="policies", extraction_text="Marine and Coastal Access Bill"),
            lx.data.Extraction(extraction_class="organizations", extraction_text="British Geological Survey"),
            lx.data.Extraction(extraction_class="measurements and facts", extraction_text="maximum tidal bed shear stresses"),
            lx.data.Extraction(extraction_class="conservation efforts", extraction_text="Marine Conservation Zones (MCZs)"),
        ]
    ),
